own \u2014 so three not revealed \u2014 you have two, so one other in play.\n\nSo B4 is still available.\n\nBut discarding B4 might help someone with [B4,B4,B4] or [B2,B3,B4] (but B2 gone) or [B4,B5,B6] (B5,B6 gone).\n\nSo no chow possible with B4 \u2014 only pung.\n\nSimilarly, B3: you have one, next player has [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 so one used. B3:2 unshown \u2014 so two left \u2014 you have one, so one other.\n\nChow with B3: [B1,B2,B3] \u2014 B1,B2 gone \u2014 impossible.\n\n[B3,B4,B5] \u2014 B4,B5 \u2014 B5 gone \u2014 impossible.\n\nSo B3 can only be used for pung.\n\nSame for B9.\n\nNow, safest discard: one that cannot be used by anyone.\n\nT7: can be used in [T5,T6,T7] \u2014 but T5,T6: T5:1 unshown, T6:1 unshown \u2014 possible.\n\n[T6,T7,T8] \u2014 T8:0 \u2014 gone.\n\n[T7,T8,T9] \u2014 T8,T9 gone.\n\nSo only possible chow: [T5,T6,T7] \u2014 T5 and T6 exist (1 each), T7:1 unshown.\n\nSo possible.\n\nSomeone might be waiting for T7.\n\nYou discarded T5 before, but not T6 or T7.\n\nNext player has [T4,T5,T6] \u2014 so they have T5,T6 \u2014 and discarded T7 \u2014 so they don't want T7.\n\nAcross player has no T.\n\nPrevious player has no T.\n\nSo T7 might be safe.\n\nBut still, possible.\n\nB9: can be used in [B7,B8,B9] \u2014 B7,B8 gone \u2014 impossible.\n\n[B8,B9,B9] \u2014 B8 gone.\n\n[B9,B9,B9] \u2014 possible for pung.\n\nBut only one unshown B9 besides yours \u2014 so hard.\n\nSimilarly, B4: only possible in pung.\n\nBut who can benefit?\n\nThe most dangerous discard is one that completes a meld.\n\nBut given that all other players have exposed four melds, they are likely waiting for a pair or single tile.\n\nSo discarding a tile that can be a pair might be dangerous.\n\nFor example, if someone is waiting for B9 to complete a pair, and they have one B9, then discarding B9 could feed them.\n\nBut B9:3 unshown \u2014 so possibly someone has one.\n\nSimilarly for B4.\n\nBut B4 is more common.\n\nNow, look at your own hand: you have two B4 and two B9.\n\nWhich is better as pair?\n\nNeither is great, but B4 is simpler, B9 is terminal.\n\nBut since you have W9 pung, you already have terminal.\n\nNow, to improve hand, you could aim for:\n\n- [W2,W3,W4] chow \u2014 need W2.\n\nW2:2 unshown \u2014 possible.\n\nThen pair B4,B4.\n\nThen need one more meld from T7, B3, B9, B9.\n\nT7,B3 \u2014 useless.\n\nOr pair B9,B9, then need meld from T7, B3, B4, B4.\n\nSame.\n\nBest chance is to keep B4 and try for B4 pung \u2014 draw B4.\n\nB4:3 unshown \u2014 so possible.\n\nSimilarly, W2 for [W2,W3,W4].\n\nW2:2 unshown.\n\nT7: only one left, less likely.\n\nSo better to keep T7 and B3 as singles, discard something else.\n\nBut you must discard one.\n\nNow, among the options, B9 is the least useful because:\n\n- Cannot form chow (no B7,B8)\n- B9 pung requires two more, only one other out there\n- While B4: we have two, one other out there, so closer to pung\n\nAlso, W3 and W4 are needed for [W2,W3,W4]\n\nT7 might be useful for [T5,T6,T7] \u2014 but T5,T6 scarce.\n\nBut you have only one T7.\n\nSo best to discard a B9.\n\nYou have two B9 \u2014 discarding one leaves a pair.\n\nWhereas if you discard B4, you have only one B4, cannot make pung.\n\nSimilarly, B3: only one, discard it, gone.\n\nSo preserve B4.\n\nThus, discard B9.\n\nReason: B9 cannot form any chow due to B7 and B8 being exhausted, and forming a pung is unlikely with only one other B9 unshown. Keeping one B9 for a pair is sufficient, and discarding one reduces hand burden. Other tiles like B4, W3, W4 are more valuable for potential chows or pungs.\n\nAnswer: Play B9"}
