3\n- Your hand: 1 W3\n- Unshown: 3 \u2192 total accounted: 1 (across) + 1 (you) = 2 \u2192 2 remaining unseen\n\nSo possible that two W3s still exist. But the valid action 'Chi W3' suggests the previous player just discarded W3, which is plausible.\n\nNow, could you use W3 to Chi? You have W3 and W4 in hand. To Chi W3, you'd need to form a sequence where W3 is one end. Possible Chows:\n- [W1, W2, W3]: need W1 & W2 \u2014 you don't have them.\n- [W2, W3, W4]: need W2 and W3 \u2014 you have W3 and W4, so if you had W2, you could do it, but you don\u2019t.\n- [W3, W4, W5]: you have W3 and W4, and W5 is in your existing meld [W4, W5, W6] \u2014 but W5 is already used in a meld.\n\nWait \u2014 important: once a tile is used in a meld, it cannot be reused. Your meld [W4, W5, W6] is exposed, so those tiles are fixed. Therefore, you cannot use W4 or W5 again for another meld. Thus, even though you have W3 and W4 in hand, your W4 is separate from the one in the meld? No \u2014 when you form a meld, you place three specific tiles into the meld area. Your hand tiles are what remain.\n\nLet\u2019s clarify:\n\nYour hand tiles: [W3:1, W4:1, B3:2, B4:2, B9:1]\n\nThat means:\n- One W3 in hand\n- One W4 in hand\n- Two B3s\n- Two B4s\n- One B9\n\nYour melds: [[W9,W9,W9], [W4,W5,W6]]\n\nSo the W4 in the meld is **not** the same as the W4 in your hand. You have two W4s total: one used in the meld, one in hand.\n\nSimilarly, W5 and W6 are in the meld, not in hand.\n\nTherefore, in hand, you have W3 and W4. If someone discards W2, you could theoretically Chi W2 to make [W2,W3,W4]. But here, the valid action is **Chi W3**, meaning someone discarded W3, and you can use it with W2 and W4 or W3 and W4 and W5.\n\nTo Chi W3, possible sequences:\n- [W1,W2,W3]: need W1 & W2 \u2014 you don\u2019t have.\n- [W2,W3,W4]: you have W3 and W4 \u2192 you would need W2 to complete this, but you don\u2019t have W2.\n- [W3,W4,W5]: you have W3 and W4, but W5 is in your existing meld \u2192 not usable.\n\nThu